NNAAMMEE
     ddeevvdd — device state change daemon

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ddeevvdd [--ddnnqq] [--ff _f_i_l_e] [--ll _n_u_m]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ddeevvdd daemon provides a way to have userland programs run when certain
     kernel events happen.

     The following options are accepted.

     --dd       Run in the foreground instead of becoming a daemon and log addi‐
              tional information for debugging.

     --ff _f_i_l_e  Use configuration file _f_i_l_e instead of the default
              _/_e_t_c_/_d_e_v_d_._c_o_n_f.  If option --ff is specified more than once, the
              last file specified is used.

     --ll _n_u_m   Limit concurrent socket connections to _n_u_m.  The default connec‐
              tion limit is 10.

     --nn       Do not process all pending events before becoming a daemon.  In‐
              stead, call daemon right away.

     --qq       Quiet mode.  Only log messages at priority LOG_WARNING or above.

IIMMPPLLEEMMEENNTTAATTIIOONN NNOOTTEESS
     The ddeevvdd utility is a system daemon that runs in the background all the
     time.  Whenever a device is added to or removed from the device tree,
     ddeevvdd will execute actions specified in devd.conf(5).  For example, ddeevvdd
     might execute dhclient(8) when an Ethernet adapter is added to the sys‐
     tem, and kill the dhclient(8) instance when the same adapter is removed.
     Another example would be for ddeevvdd to use a table to locate and load via
     kldload(8) the proper driver for an unrecognized device that is added to
     the system.

     The ddeevvdd utility hooks into the devctl(4) device driver.  This device
     driver has hooks into the device configuration system.  When nodes are
     added or deleted from the tree, this device will deliver information
     about the event to ddeevvdd.  Once ddeevvdd has parsed the message, it will
     search its action list for that kind of event and perform the action with
     the highest matching value.  For most mundane uses, the default handlers
     are adequate.  However, for more advanced users, the power is present to
     tweak every aspect of what happens.

     The ddeevvdd utility reads _/_e_t_c_/_d_e_v_d_._c_o_n_f or the alternate configuration file
     specified with a --ff option and uses that file to drive the rest of the
     process.  While the format of this file is described in devd.conf(5),
     some basics are covered here.

     In the ooppttiioonnss section, one can define multiple directories to search for
     config files.  The default config file specifies _/_e_t_c_/_d_e_v_d and
     _/_u_s_r_/_l_o_c_a_l_/_e_t_c_/_d_e_v_d as directories to search.  All files in these direc‐
     tories whose names match the pattern _*_._c_o_n_f are parsed.  These files are
     intended to be installed by third party vendors that wish to hook into
     the ddeevvdd system without modifying the user's other config files.

     Since devctl(4) allows only one active reader, ddeevvdd multiplexes it, for‐
     warding all events to any number of connected clients.  Clients connect
     by opening the SOCK_SEQPACKET UNIX domain socket at
     _/_v_a_r_/_r_u_n_/_d_e_v_d_._s_e_q_p_a_c_k_e_t_._p_i_p_e.

FFIILLEESS
     /etc/devd.conf                The default ddeevvdd configuration file.
     /var/run/devd.seqpacket.pipe  The socket used by ddeevvdd to communicate with
                                   its clients.
     /var/run/devd.pipe            A deprecated socket retained for use with
                                   old clients.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     devctl(4), devd.conf(5)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The ddeevvdd utility first appeared in FreeBSD 5.0.

